course: dictionary
lesson: 579
title: Autistic: a different way of experiencing the world
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Autistic» describes a person who has autism. Autism is a lifelong difference in how the brain works. It affects how a person communicates, how they make sense of the world, and how they feel sounds, lights, touch and other senses.
explain: «Autistic people experience the world differently, and loud noise or changes can be hard for them.» A difference is not the same as a problem to be fixed. An autistic classmate may have many strengths, and may need a quiet space or a warning before a change.
explain: Some autistic children flap their hands, rock or hum. This is called stimming, and it can help them calm down or enjoy themselves. Being kind and patient helps everyone. Laughing at someone for it makes their stress much worse.
fact: Autism is a lifelong difference in how the brain works, so a person who is autistic is autistic all their life.
fact: «Autistic person» and «autistic child» are normal, respectful ways to describe someone.
fact: Autistic people may find loud noise, crowds or sudden changes hard. Some like routines and have strong special interests.
fact: Stimming, such as flapping hands, rocking or humming, is a way to calm down or enjoy yourself.
fact: Many classrooms have quiet spaces and visual timetables to help autistic pupils feel safe and ready to learn.
exercise: What does «autistic» describe? | A person who cannot hear at all | *A person who has autism, a lifelong difference in how the brain works | A very loud type of noise | A person who is always shy
exercise: «An autistic classmate may find loud noise or sudden changes hard.» Which of these might be hard for an autistic classmate? | A familiar routine they know well | *Loud noise | A quiet room with soft lights | A book they chose to read
exercise: «Many autistic children flap their hands, rock or hum.» What is this called? | *Stimming | Shouting | Sleeping | Hiding
test: «A girl kept laughing at an autistic classmate who rocked when upset.» What is the kind thing to do instead? | *Be patient and kind, and give them space to calm down | Join in the laughing | Film them to share with friends | Shout at them to stop
test: Why do many classrooms have quiet spaces? | For playing loud games | *To help autistic pupils calm down when noise is too much | To store the spare books | To hide children from the teacher
exam: Which statement about autism is true? | Autism is only a problem in bad weather | Autism goes away when you grow up | Autism is caught from other children | *Autism is a lifelong difference in how the brain works
